### Body

## Summary

As of March 25, 2026, we are unable to enable auto-merge on pull requests until **all** PR requirements (required checks, approvals, etc.) have been fulfilled. Previously, we could enable auto-merge immediately after opening a PR, and it would automatically merge once all requirements were met — which is the documented and expected behavior.

This is an undocumented behavior change. The [official documentation](https://docs.github.com/en/pull-requests/collaborating-with-pull-requests/incorporating-changes-from-a-pull-request/automatically-merging-a-pull-request) still states:

> If you enable auto-merge for a pull request, the pull request will merge automatically when all required reviews are met and all required status checks have passed.

And:

> The option to enable auto-merge is shown only on pull requests that cannot be merged immediately.

This clearly describes the **old behavior**: enable auto-merge *before* requirements are met, and it merges *when* they are met.

## Error Details

When attempting to enable auto-merge via the GitHub web UI, the following request is made:

**Request:**

```
POST /<owner>/<repo>/pull/<number>/page_data/enable_auto_merge
{"mergeMethod":"GROUP"}
```

**Response (HTTP 422):**

```
{"error":"Failed enabling auto-merge for pull request"}
```

The `mergeMethod: GROUP` indicates we are using a **merge queue** (configured via repository rulesets).

## Ruleset Configuration

We are using **repository rulesets only** (no classic branch protection rules). Here is our complete ruleset configuration for the default branch, retrieved via the API (`GET /repos/{owner}/{repo}/rulesets/{id}`):

```
